## Summary

Choosing stretch in the Tempo Operations window and pressing Apply rejects a valid edit selection instead of stretching its tempo. The window sends a parameter the command does not use to specify a stretch. The same operation succeeds when called directly with its documented factor or length parameter.

## Runtime or environment

SoundCraft commit d7fc5dc383837b1059ee984cd535eb745dabaee7. Verification used the CLI built from this source, a blank synthetic session at 48000 Hz, and a 120 BPM tempo map. The window dispatch was traced in source; the GUI was not launched.

## Steps to reproduce

The window steps below were verified by tracing their source dispatch. The resulting engine payload was independently executed with the headless CLI.

1. In a blank session at 48000 Hz and 120 BPM, set the edit selection from sample 0 through sample 48000.
2. Open Event > Tempo Operations > Tempo Operations Window.
3. Choose stretch in the Curve list and set both BPM fields to 120.
4. Press Apply. The operation rejects the request with the factor/length error and leaves the tempo map unchanged.

To replay the exact window payload without launching the GUI, save the following commands as `replay.txt` and run `SOUNDCRAFT_NO_PREFS=1 soundcraft-cli script replay.txt --keep-going`. The flag permits the state query and successful control to run after the rejected command; a zero overall exit status does not mean every command succeeded.

```text
session.new {"name":"Synthetic","sample_rate":48000}
event.tempo {"bpm":120}
edit.select {"start":0,"end":48000}
event.tempo_ops_window {}
event.tempo_stretch {"end_bpm":120}
event.tempo_ops_window {}
event.tempo_stretch {"factor":2}
event.tempo_ops_window {}
```

